[high, perf/concurrency] gix-store/src/kind.rs — index maintenance makes every entity write O(N) and serializes all writers of a kind. Put::at (kind.rs:740), try_update (kind.rs:266), remove (kind.rs:506), and anonymous_at (kind.rs:798) each call source_entries() — a full prefix scan of every entity ref of the kind — and rewrite the entire index tree, then CAS the single shared refs/cache/<kind>/index-v1 ref. Two writers putting *different* entities of the same kind now conflict on the index ref: one gets LostRace and retries, re-scanning all N refs and re-writing an N-entry tree each attempt. Before the index feature these writes were independent single-ref CASes. For a kind with many (e.g. anonymous) entities this is quadratic aggregate write cost and a throughput collapse under concurrency. Consider: maintain the index lazily/best-effort (e.g. only in rebuild_index), tolerate staleness via the existing read validation, or shard it.
